---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { LICENSE_CLAUSE } from '../../data/product-facts.mjs';

const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'TechArticle',
  headline: 'Install mod_pagespeed 2.1 from packages.modpagespeed.com (apt + yum)',
  description:
    'Signed apt and yum repository for the mod_pagespeed 2.1 Apache and nginx modules on Debian 11/12/13, Ubuntu 22.04/24.04, and AlmaLinux/RHEL/Rocky 9 and 10. One-line install configures the repo and pulls the package.',
  about: 'mod_pagespeed 2.1',
  image: 'https://modpagespeed.com/og-default.png?v=2',
  author: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'We-Amp B.V.' },
  publisher: {
    '@type': 'Organization',
    name: 'We-Amp B.V.',
    logo: {
      '@type': 'ImageObject',
      url: 'https://modpagespeed.com/logo.svg',
    },
  },
  url: 'https://modpagespeed.com/download/apt-yum/',
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Install mod_pagespeed 2.1 on Apache & nginx via apt / yum"
  description="Signed apt and yum repo for the mod_pagespeed 2.1 Apache and nginx modules. One line installs it on Debian 11/12/13, Ubuntu 22.04/24.04, and AlmaLinux/RHEL/Rocky 9 and 10."
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <section class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-16">
    <p class="eyebrow text-interactive">mod_pagespeed 2.1</p>
    <h1 class="h1-hero mt-2 text-text-body">
      Install from <code class="font-mono">packages.modpagespeed.com</code>
    </h1>
    <p class="lede mt-6">
      A signed apt and yum repository for the mod_pagespeed 2.1 Apache and nginx modules. One line
      detects the distro, installs the GPG signing key, writes the source file, and pulls the
      package. The nginx module ships prebuilt for Debian 11/12/13 and Ubuntu 22.04/24.04 (amd64 and
      arm64), and for RHEL/AlmaLinux/Rocky 9 (x86_64, aarch64) and 10 (x86_64), each pinned to that
      distro's stock nginx.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-10 rounded-2xl border border-border bg-bg-secondary p-6">
      <p class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-text-annotation">Install</p>
      <pre
        class="mt-3 overflow-x-auto rounded bg-stone-900 dark:bg-stone-950 p-4 text-sm text-stone-100"><code data-umami-event="cta_download_apt_yum_oneliner">curl -fsSL https://packages.modpagespeed.com/install.sh | sudo sh</code></pre>
      <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-text-muted">Then install the module for your server. Apache:</p>
      <pre
        class="mt-2 overflow-x-auto rounded bg-stone-900 dark:bg-stone-950 p-4 text-sm text-stone-100"><code># Debian 11/12/13, Ubuntu 22.04/24.04
sudo apt-get install mod-pagespeed

# AlmaLinux / RHEL / Rocky 9 and 10
sudo dnf install mod-pagespeed</code></pre>
      <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-text-muted">nginx:</p>
      <pre
        class="mt-2 overflow-x-auto rounded bg-stone-900 dark:bg-stone-950 p-4 text-sm text-stone-100"><code># Debian 11/12/13, Ubuntu 22.04/24.04
sudo apt-get install nginx-module-pagespeed

# AlmaLinux / RHEL / Rocky 9 and 10
sudo dnf install nginx-module-pagespeed</code></pre>
      <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-text-muted">
        Installing <code class="font-mono">nginx-module-pagespeed</code> drops a modules-enabled auto-include
        snippet — there is nothing to copy by hand. Each <code class="font-mono">.so</code> is pinned
        to its distro's exact stock nginx version (nginx's <code class="font-mono"
          >--with-compat</code
        > does not relax that check), so upgrading nginx past the stock version needs a matching rebuild
        — <a href="/contact/" class="text-interactive underline">contact us</a>
        for a pinned build. This is also the prebuilt fix <a
          href="/blog/ngx-pagespeed-wont-build-modern-nginx/"
          class="text-interactive hover:text-interactive-hover underline"
          >if your ngx_pagespeed build is failing</a
        > on a modern nginx in the first place.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2 class="h2-section mt-14 text-text-body">Supported today</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-sm font-semibold text-text-body">
      nginx (<code class="font-mono">nginx-module-pagespeed</code>)
    </p>
    <ul class="mt-2 space-y-2 text-text-body">
      <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
        <span class="mt-1 flex-shrink-0 text-stone-400 font-bold">&bull;</span>
        <span>Debian 11 (bullseye), amd64 + arm64, stock nginx 1.18.0</span>
      </li>
      <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
        <span class="mt-1 flex-shrink-0 text-stone-400 font-bold">&bull;</span>
        <span>Debian 12 (bookworm), amd64 + arm64, stock nginx 1.22.1</span>
      </li>
      <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
        <span class="mt-1 flex-shrink-0 text-stone-400 font-bold">&bull;</span>
        <span>Debian 13 (trixie), amd64 + arm64, stock nginx 1.26.3</span>
      </li>
      <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
        <span class="mt-1 flex-shrink-0 text-stone-400 font-bold">&bull;</span>
        <span>Ubuntu 22.04 (jammy), amd64 + arm64, stock nginx 1.18.0</span>
      </li>
      <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
        <span class="mt-1 flex-shrink-0 text-stone-400 font-bold">&bull;</span>
        <span>Ubuntu 24.04 (noble), amd64 + arm64, stock nginx 1.24.0</span>
      </li>
      <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
        <span class="mt-1 flex-shrink-0 text-stone-400 font-bold">&bull;</span>
        <span>AlmaLinux 9 / RHEL 9 / Rocky 9 (el9), x86_64 + aarch64, stock nginx 1.20.1</span>
      </li>
      <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
        <span class="mt-1 flex-shrink-0 text-stone-400 font-bold">&bull;</span>
        <span>AlmaLinux 10 / RHEL 10 / Rocky 10 (el10), x86_64</span>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p class="mt-6 text-sm font-semibold text-text-body">
      Apache (<code class="font-mono">mod-pagespeed</code>)
    </p>
    <ul class="mt-2 space-y-2 text-text-body">
      <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
        <span class="mt-1 flex-shrink-0 text-stone-400 font-bold">&bull;</span>
        <span>Debian 11 (bullseye), amd64 + arm64</span>
      </li>
      <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
        <span class="mt-1 flex-shrink-0 text-stone-400 font-bold">&bull;</span>
        <span>Debian 12 (bookworm), amd64 + arm64</span>
      </li>
      <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
        <span class="mt-1 flex-shrink-0 text-stone-400 font-bold">&bull;</span>
        <span>Debian 13 (trixie), amd64 + arm64</span>
      </li>
      <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
        <span class="mt-1 flex-shrink-0 text-stone-400 font-bold">&bull;</span>
        <span>Ubuntu 22.04 (jammy), amd64 + arm64</span>
      </li>
      <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
        <span class="mt-1 flex-shrink-0 text-stone-400 font-bold">&bull;</span>
        <span>Ubuntu 24.04 (noble), amd64 + arm64</span>
      </li>
      <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
        <span class="mt-1 flex-shrink-0 text-stone-400 font-bold">&bull;</span>
        <span>AlmaLinux 9 / RHEL 9 / Rocky 9 (el9), x86_64 + aarch64</span>
      </li>
      <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
        <span class="mt-1 flex-shrink-0 text-stone-400 font-bold">&bull;</span>
        <span>AlmaLinux 10 / RHEL 10 / Rocky 10 (el10), x86_64</span>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-text-annotation">
      EL8 is no longer in the generic yum repo. <a
        href="/contact/"
        class="text-interactive underline">Contact us</a
      > if you run an nginx version we don't yet package and need a pinned build.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-text-annotation">
      cPanel / EasyApache 4: the <code class="font-mono">ea-apache24-mod_pagespeed</code> package covers
      EL8 and EL9 (x86_64) and auto-enables on cPanel hosts. See the <a
        href="/docs/cpanel/"
        class="text-interactive underline">cPanel guide</a
      >.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-text-annotation">
      Running ngx_pagespeed? See <a
        href="https://ngxpagespeed.com/"
        class="text-interactive underline">ngxpagespeed.com</a
      >.
    </p>

    <h2 class="h2-section mt-14 text-text-body">How it works</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-text-body">
      The install script detects the distribution, fetches the public signing key (<code
        class="font-mono">F50D6054F10712A0</code
      >), and writes a single source file:
      <code class="font-mono">/etc/apt/sources.list.d/modpagespeed.list</code> on apt, or
      <code class="font-mono">/etc/yum.repos.d/modpagespeed.repo</code> on yum. Credentials and URLs stay
      out of the source file itself — apt credentials live in
      <code class="font-mono">/etc/apt/auth.conf.d/</code>; the yum file is mode <code>0600</code>.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 text-text-body">
      Subsequent <code class="font-mono">apt-get upgrade</code> /
      <code class="font-mono">dnf upgrade</code> picks up new releases automatically.
    </p>

    <h2 class="h2-section mt-14 text-text-body">License</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-text-body">
      The package is the same mod_pagespeed 2.1 build as the direct download links. Install it and
      it optimizes right away, including <a
        href="/blog/nginx-image-optimization-module/"
        class="text-interactive hover:text-interactive-hover underline"
        >automatic WebP image optimization via the nginx module</a
      >. mod_pagespeed 2.1 is {LICENSE_CLAUSE} — free in development and in production; what We-Amp sells
      is <a href="/pricing/" class="text-interactive underline"
        >support &mdash; and hardened builds</a
      >. First-time setup is covered in the <a
        href="/docs/getting-started/"
        class="text-interactive underline">getting-started guide</a
      >; see <a href="/download/" class="text-interactive underline">/download/</a> for the full integration
      matrix (Apache, nginx, IIS, ASP.NET Core).
    </p>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
